  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving children&apos;s education and youth employment</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Coffee is one of the top export products of Uganda. 95% of the coffee exported from Uganda is Robusta coffee, making it the number five exporting country of Robusta in the world. About 90% of coffee farms in Uganda are smallholder farmers having farms of one to two acre. The Uganda coffee sector has a high-risk profile on the occurrence of child labour. The main underlying reason for child labour is poverty. Families are not able to earn a living income, and school fees and other household costs cannot be paid.In the coffee sector, where relevant interventions have not yet taken place, about 40% of the households keep children away from school to work on their farm. With the support of project activities including mainly awareness raising, improved incomes and community-based, the incidence of child labour can be reduced to less than 5%.In addition, coffee farmers do not have diversified crops and are insufficiently prepared for the effects of climate change. In April &#x2013; July 2022, a long period of drought took place in Uganda, destroying the crops of maize and beans, resulting in an increase of food prices by more than 50%. Farmer input are twice as expensive due to the Russian &#x2013; Ukrainian war.Recent studies also show that the percentage of child labour has further increased during and after COVID-19 pandemic. Local and national government fail to make progress in diminishing these percentages by taking corrective and preventive action against child labour. A positive note is that the severity of child labour is not classified at the highest level. Hazardous or forced child labour does not seem to be common in the coffee sector.The impact assessment of Brand Coffee Farm has led to the following main interventions:&#x2022;	Improving household income through increased coffee yield, diversification of income streams (intercropping cash crops, mushroom breeding and beekeeping), application of home-made organic fertilizer, compost and organic pesticides;&#x2022;	Youth employment through the start of youth start-up businesses;&#x2022;	Cooperative oriented approach to mitigate, remediate &amp; preventing child labour;&#x2022;	Improving the quality of education;&#x2022;	Mobilizing support of local government. The project is currently working on all of the above areas and creating the first successes on the ground. There are eight youth start-ups active in both cooperative areas and school- and community structures have been initiated, revitalised and trained. In 2025 Brand Development Group will continue to support the youth to work and train small holder farmers to ensure household incomes are being improved. The first results of the project can be noticed in the participating schools and communities regarding an improved awareness of the importance of children&#x2019;s education and parents facilitating their children to stay in school.</narrative>
  </description>
